messages:
sara: Hey all — quick Q about the Node/TS SDK on edge runtimes. We're seeing intermittent auth failures when bundling for Next Edge (middleware & app router edge). Our SDK currently reads process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY which works in server Node builds, but in Edge the env vars are replaced at build time and it ends up undefined in some deployments. Also streaming responses sometimes arrive as incompatible stream types in Vite SSR. Anyone seen similar and have a recommended pattern?

mike: Yup saw this with Vercel Edge. Pattern that worked for us: don't rely on process.env at runtime in edge bundles. Pass the key from a secure server entrypoint or set a request header from your middleware so the edge function receives it as a header. E.g. set `x-rw-api-key` in middleware and read `req.headers.get('x-rw-api-key')` in the edge handler. :+1:

jung: Also fwiw, the SDK has a smaller edge-compatible entrypoint to avoid Node-only deps. We import it like: `import { createClientForEdge } from '@redwood-inference/sdk/edge'` — that avoids bringing in fs/child_process shims that break some bundlers. If you can't use that, tree-shake the main bundle and ensure `package.json` exports field points to an ESM build.

carla: We weren't using the edge entrypoint originally either. Snippet that fixed auth + streaming for us (Next Edge API route):

```ts
// app/api/redwood/route/route.ts (edge runtime)
import { createClientForEdge } from '@redwood-inference/sdk/edge'

export const runtime = 'edge'

export default async function handler(req: Request) {
  const key = req.headers.get('x-rw-api-key') || process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RW_FALLBACK
  if (!key) return new Response('unauthorized', { status: 401 })

  const client = createClientForEdge({ apiKey: key })
  const stream = await client.generateStream({ prompt: 'hi' })
  return new Response(stream) // stream is a Web ReadableStream in Edge
}
```

sara: This is really helpful, thanks. One follow-up on streaming: in Vite dev/middleware we sometimes get a Node Readable instead of a Web ReadableStream, which breaks `res.body.getReader()`. Any good cross-runtime helpers?

mike: We wrote a tiny helper to normalize: if `typeof (stream as any).getReader === 'function'` treat as WebStream; else if `stream instanceof require('stream').Readable` convert via `stream.Readable.toWeb(nodeStream)` (Node 18+). Pseudocode: 

```ts
function toWebReadable(stream: any) {
  if (!stream) return null
  if (typeof stream.getReader === 'function') return stream
  const { Readable } = require('stream')
  if (stream instanceof Readable) return Readable.toWeb(stream)
  return stream
}
```

Note: bundlers sometimes polyfill `stream` and `Readable.toWeb` may not exist — guard for that.

jung: Another gotcha: retries + streaming. If you have automatic retries at the network layer, you can end up with duplicate tokens emitted on retry. We added two mitigations: 1) only retry non-streaming requests, 2) attach an idempotency header `x-redwood-idempotency` for safe server-side retries.

